    thinking: "**Analyzing ScheduleView.tsx**\n\nI\'ve got a firm grasp on `ScheduleView.tsx` now. My focus is on aesthetic upgrades, specifically incorporating a Noir Slate theme for a premium look. I\'m starting by examining the root container at line 266, which uses a `flex-1` and `overflow-y` class combination, and what adjustments will lead to the desired look.\n\n\n**Refining Theming Details**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the finer aesthetic points, starting with the `<div id=\"schedule-container\">`. The goal is a Noir Slate theme, so I\'m updating its background to `#0c0d14`. Next, I\'m refactoring the sub-header bar. I\'m focusing on the Outlet selector circle; I\'m upgrading it with a stylish look: `w-6 h-6 rounded-lg bg-rose-500/15 text-rose-450 border border-rose-500/25 flex items-center justify-center text-[11px] font-black tracking-tight`. I\'m also styling the toggle list to be a matte, dark shape with active/inactive states. Finally, I will adjust the main course categories selector\'s styling.\n\n\n**Styling Button Elements**\n\nI\'m now detailing styles for various interactive elements. Button states are defined; active buttons will have a `bg-rose-500 text-white shadow` appearance, inactive buttons get a `text-zinc-400 hover:text-[#fff] hover:bg-white/5` look. I\'ve also nailed the \"\345\217\257\347\272\246\" switch with its background colors and text properties, and the month calendar circle button\'s visual states. I\'m also styling the scrolling dates carousel and the filter row.\n\n\n**Defining Empty Stage Appearance**\n\nI\'m now detailing the Empty Stage appearance in `ScheduleView.tsx`. I\'ve outlined a container with a dark `#13141f` background and rounded edges. Inside, I\'m styling an icon wrapper, the text for message titles/subtitles, and a refresh button with a rose-colored glow. The classes are: `bg-[#13141f] rounded-[28px] border border-white/5 text-center flex flex-col items-center justify-center p-8 shadow-2xl`, and the relevant classes for the icon wrapper, text, and button.\n\n\n\n**Styling Elements\' Details**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on detailing the component elements within the schedule view. I\'ve defined specific styles for avatar elements with rounded borders and overlaid rating badges, and text labels with a focus on instructor names and booking information. I also have the details for the side action buttons to reflect the status of the classes. These styles incorporate specific color palettes and font weights.\n\nI am also focusing on the classes selection button.\n\n\n**Defining Button and Grid Styles**\n\nI\'m now detailing the component element styles. Specifically, I have defined a standard booking CTA button and a waitlist gradient button. I\'m also now styling the weekly grid elements within ScheduleView.tsx. I am focusing on the weekly header navigator and navigation arrows, along with the outer grid container and its cells.\n\n\n**Specifying Component Styles**\n\nI\'m now detailing component styles within the grid elements. The header\'s got a rose-tinted border, with text highlights for day numbers. Hourly time markers are styled for clarity. Inner grid slots have empty/occupied states, using distinct container and card styles. Occupied slots incorporate colored genre categories. I\'m focusing on hover/active states now.\n\n\n**Refining Month Calendar**\n\nI\'m now detailing the styling for the month calendar selection dialog. I\'ve designed a dark backdrop with a blur effect and a modal container with rounded edges, a shadow, and border. The header\'s got a darker background and border. I\'ve got the day name headers, grid clickable dates, their states, and the footer\'s close button styling ready. My goal is to use the `bg-black/80 backdrop-blur-xs` and other classes to achieve the sleek look.\n\n\n**Styling Slider Elements**\n\nI\'m now detailing the course selector filter drawer. I\'ve styled the backdrop and main slider container, using a dark, blurred backdrop with rounded edges and a shadow. The header includes title and section headings, and I\'ve defined styles for tag buttons: active tags are rose-colored and highlighted, while inactive ones have a subtle hover effect. The bottom command drawer buttons complete the design.\n\n\n"
